/page A4 --margin 2cm
/font "Geist" --google "wght@400;500;600;700" --body --heading

# Pagination in Polyester

Polyester decides where one page ends and the next begins for you. The rules
are conservative: the same source produces the same page boundaries in the
live preview and in the rendered PDF, with no manual layout work.

This document is itself paginated A4 — every behavior described below is
visible as you scroll through the rendered output.

## Page setup

Every paginated document starts with a `/page` declaration:

/code polyester {
  /page A4 --margin 2cm
}

Supported sizes: A3, A4, A5, Letter, Legal. Add `--landscape` for landscape
orientation. The margin can be in any CSS length unit (`2cm`, `1in`, `24pt`).

## Pageless mode

If you want one long, continuous document — no automatic page breaks at all —
declare `--pageless`:

/code polyester {
  /page --pageless
}

In pageless mode the document flows as a single tall page. Use `/pagebreak`
when you want to force a break.

## Manual page breaks

`/pagebreak` ends the current page. It works in both paginated and pageless
documents.

/code polyester {
  # Cover

  /pagebreak

  # Body

  This starts on a new page.
}

/pagebreak

# Automatic pagination

In paginated mode, Polyester walks your content top to bottom and drops it
onto pages. When the next block does not fit, the current page ends and the
block moves to a new one. Several rules govern *how* the break happens.

## Paragraphs stay whole

A paragraph that does not fit on the current page is moved entirely to the
next page rather than being cut in half. You will not see a paragraph end
with a stranded word at the bottom of one page and resume at the top of the
next.

If a paragraph is so long that it cannot fit on a single page even by itself
(rare for body copy, possible for very long quotes), Polyester splits it —
but only at sentence boundaries, never mid-sentence, and never in a way
that leaves a single sentence dangling on either side.

## Headings stay with their content

When the next block (a paragraph, a list, a table, a region) does not fit
and gets pushed to a new page, any heading that was just placed at the
bottom of the current page travels with it. A heading is never left orphaned
at the end of a page with its content stranded on the next.

## Lists split cleanly

Lists are the one block type that Polyester *will* split across pages: items
flow onto the current page until one does not fit, then the remaining items
continue on the next page. Numbering is preserved for ordered lists.

If a single list item is too tall to fit on a page, the whole list moves to
the next page first, in case more room helps.

/pagebreak

# Fonts and deterministic pagination

A document only paginates the same way in the live preview, in the rendered
PDF, and in any browser that opens the HTML if every environment renders
text with the same glyph metrics. That means the same font.

Relying on installed system fonts is fragile: your machine has them, the PDF
renderer might not, and a reader's browser definitely will not. The result
is that "where the page breaks" depends on whose computer is looking at it.

The fix is `/font`. Declare the font you want once, and Polyester downloads
it (or reads it from disk), base64-inlines it into the document, and uses
the same bytes everywhere.

## Google Fonts

/code polyester {
  /font "Geist" --google "wght@400;500;700" --body --heading
  /font "JetBrains Mono" --google --mono
}

The `--google` value is the Google Fonts axis spec — `wght@400;700` for two
weights, `ital,wght@0,400;1,400` for italic + roman at the same weight, and
so on. Omit it to get the default 400 weight.

`--body`, `--heading`, and `--mono` set the registered family as the default
for body text, headings, and code respectively.

## Local files

/code polyester {
  /font "MyBrand" --src "fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2"
  /font "MyBrand" --src "fonts/MyBrand-Bold.woff2" --weight 700
}

Paths are resolved relative to the document. WOFF2, WOFF, TTF, and OTF
files all work; WOFF2 produces the smallest output.

Once a font is fetched the bytes are cached on disk, so subsequent builds
are offline-friendly and finish in milliseconds.

/pagebreak

# Choosing a page mode

/columns 2 {
  /card --title "Use paginated when:" {
    /list {
      Output is a printed or saved PDF
      Content has natural section breaks
      Page numbers, headers, or covers matter
      You want predictable per-page layout
    }
  }

  /card --title "Use pageless when:" {
    /list {
      Output is a long-form web page
      Tables or figures must never split
      Manual page-break placement is enough
      You want one long scroll
    }
  }
}

# What you will not see

A short list of things Polyester actively prevents:

/list {
  A paragraph cut off mid-sentence
  A single word stranded at the top or bottom of a page
  A heading at the bottom of a page with its paragraph on the next
  Different page boundaries between live preview and rendered PDF
  Content that silently disappears because it did not fit
}

If a block genuinely cannot fit on any page (a region with a fixed height
larger than the page, an image taller than the content area), Polyester
flags it with a red dashed outline rather than dropping it. That is your
cue to resize the content or change the page settings.
